35,2,1,talk,Servas,,"My findings suggest a shift in our human resources. While we have a strong glasshouse crew, we are lacking phenotyping technicians, and our senior modelling analysts are currently abroad. We must consider this.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Midori,,"That is very helpful, Servas. I found that while computing hours are now free, animal-unit day rates have risen sharply. Also, shipping costs are up, though there is a subsidy for glasshouse space.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"I found some worrying things! The spectroscopy suite was broken for eleven weeks because of a contract issue, and there's only one engineer for three institutes. But the animal facility is doing great now!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears the environment is quite volatile. Field sites are suffering from natural disasters, and projects relying on live breeding colonies are significantly more prone to schedule overruns than computational ones.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"I found that catalogue-style projects have provided long-term value, whereas mechanism-focused ones often remain unresolved. Additionally, the national repository's turnaround time has improved significantly, making data deposits much faster.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Midori, @Kenji, it seems the glasshouse is our strongest asset right now, both in staffing and subsidies. However, the lack of phenotyping technicians and the spectroscopy issues are concerning for certain types of research.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Servas, you're right. Between the subsidies and the crew strength, the glasshouse seems very stable. It's a relief to have some certainty, though the animal costs and staffing gaps are worrying.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"Yeah, the animal facility is doing great with the new vet cover, but that spectroscopy problem is scary! If a project needs that, they might be stuck for a long time.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a fascinating intersection of risks. We have a fragile field environment and a precarious spectroscopy suite, contrasted with a robust glasshouse and a highly efficient national repository. Stability seems to reside indoors.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"The data suggests a clear pattern. We should prioritize projects that produce long-term utility, such as catalogues, and avoid those reliant on fragile instrumentation or high-risk biological colonies that often fail to resolve their central questions.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Servas,,"@Toshio, your point about long-term utility is wise. If we combine that with the current stability of the glasshouse and the repository, we can find a project that is both sustainable and impactful.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Midori,,"I agree. It seems we should look for a project that avoids the rising costs of animal units and the risks of field sites, while taking advantage of the glasshouse and free computing.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Kenji,,I agree! We should definitely pick something that doesn't need that broken spectroscopy machine or the field sites that keep getting flooded. Let's find a project that can actually get its work done!,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely. We are looking for a proposal that minimizes exposure to environmental volatility and biological unpredictability, while maximizing the use of our currently stable infrastructure and efficient data pipelines. Logic dictates we avoid the ""high-risk"" categories.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"I agree. We should favor a proposal that aligns with the current strengths of our infrastructure and has a historical track record of delivering tangible, lasting results rather than theoretical promises.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Servas,,"We have reached a consensus on our criteria. We seek a project with long-term utility that leverages our stable glasshouse and repository, while avoiding animal costs, field risks, and fragile instrumentation. I am ready for the candidates.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Midori,,I agree completely. It feels like we have a very clear set of guidelines now. I'm looking forward to seeing which of the candidates fits these criteria best. I am ready as well.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm ready too! Let's see who the candidates are and find the one that fits our plan perfectly!,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,The stage is set. We have a coherent framework for evaluation; now we simply need to apply it to the proposals. I am ready to see the candidates.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Toshio,,I agree. We have established a rational set of constraints. I am ready to evaluate the candidates against these findings.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
